  {
    "id": "doc_patents_act_guidelines_chunk_1",
    "document": "Section 3(p) of the Indian Patents Act 1970 mandates that 'an invention which in effect is traditional knowledge or which is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of traditionally known component or components' is NOT an invention within the meaning of this Act.\nExamination Standard: Examiners cite CSIR's Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) containing over 450,000 formulations from classical texts (Caraka Samhita, Susruta Samhita, Astanga Hrdaya, Siddha Vaidya Thirattu, Unani Qarabadeen).\nAny attempt to claim a plant extract, raw herbal powder, or traditional concoction for its documented traditional indication is automatically rejected under Section 3(p).",
    "metadata": {
      "document_id": "doc_patents_act_guidelines",
      "document_name": "Indian Patents Act 1970 - Sections 3(p), 3(e), 3(d) Examination Guidelines",
      "page": 5,
      "section": "Section 3(p) - Traditional Knowledge Exclusion Bar",
      "document_type": "PDF",
      "source": "CGPDTM_Ayurveda_Patent_Guidelines_2023.pdf",
      "upload_date": "2026-09-20T09:57:12.637Z"
